    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Founded in 1232 by Muhammad I ibn al-Ahmar, it survived as a tributary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> while the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> fell.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">Silk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and dried fruit exported through Málaga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, often by Genoese merchants, sustained a crowded mountain realm.</li><li>Civil wars and the Castilian war of 1482–1492 ended it when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-xii-of-granada">Boabdil</a> surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> on 2 January 1492.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> are inscribed with poems by the court poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-zamrak">Ibn Zamrak</a>, some of which speak in the voice of the building itself.</p></aside>
    </section>

