    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hud"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-hud.jpg" alt="Ibn Hud" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hud">Ibn Hud</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ibn_Hud,_Cantigas_de_Santa_Maria_169.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Under the patronage of Alfonso X, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> was born from the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-las-navas-de-tolosa">Battle of Las Navas de Tolosa</a> in 1212. In the turmoil that followed, local strongmen competed for power, the most successful being <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-hud">Ibn Hud</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murcia">Murcia</a>. In 1232 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-i-of-granada">Muhammad ibn Yusuf ibn Nasr</a>, a minor noble from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arjona">Arjona</a> known as Ibn al-Ahmar, proclaimed himself ruler in his home town. He soon won <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaen">Jaén</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadix">Guadix</a>, entered Granada in 1237 or 1238, and after the death of his rival Ibn Hud in 1238 gained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> and Málaga. In Granada he began turning the fortress on the Sabika hill above the city into the palace-city of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-iii-of-castile">Ferdinand III of Castile</a> besieged Jaén in 1246, Muhammad I surrendered the city, recognised Ferdinand as his lord and agreed to pay an annual tribute; he even sent troops to help <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> take <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> in 1248. This calculated submission allowed his dynasty, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrids</a> or Banu al-Ahmar, to survive while the rest of al-Andalus fell. The new state stretched along the mountains and coast of the south-east, from the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a> to beyond Almería, shielded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sierra-nevada-spain">Sierra Nevada</a>, and it received waves of refugees from Seville, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> and Murcia, becoming the last refuge of Andalusi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/granada"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/granada.jpg" alt="Granada" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Granada_(25987961022).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Brett Hodnett, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a> was small, mountainous and densely populated, its numbers swollen by Muslims leaving the lands conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>; estimates of its population vary widely, but the capital was probably one of the larger cities of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Unlike earlier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, the population was almost entirely Muslim and Arabic-speaking, with a small Jewish community and some Christian merchants and captives. Intensive irrigated farming in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vega-of-granada">Vega of Granada</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alpujarras">Alpujarras</a> produced cereals, fruit, olives and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> cane, while mulberry trees supported a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> industry whose cloths were prized across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. Málaga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> and Granada exported silk, dried figs and raisins, almonds and sugar and imported grain, often through Genoese merchants who kept a powerful presence in Málaga, and trade also ran across the Strait to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s gold coins, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid</a> <em>dinars</em> or doblas, circulated widely. Religious life followed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> law, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufi</a> brotherhoods and frontier mosques flourished. Yusuf I founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa-of-granada">Madrasa of Granada</a> in 1349, and the city's scholars, judges and secretaries kept alive the literary culture of al-Andalus. Constant raiding on the frontier also made the ransoming and exchange of captives a regular part of life on both sides.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado.jpg" alt="Battle of Río Salado" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado">Battle of Río Salado</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:La_batalla_del_Salado_(1340).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown artist, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>'s survival depended on fortification, diplomacy and help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>. Its frontier with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> was guarded by hilltop castles and chains of watchtowers, and its towns, set in rugged terrain, were difficult to besiege. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s own forces relied on light cavalry riding in the short-stirruped <em>jineta</em> style, so effective that Castilian horsemen adopted it, alongside crossbowmen and infantry levies. From 1275 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid</a> sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> sent armies across the Strait, and a corps of North African volunteers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volunteers-of-the-faith">Ghuzat al-Jihad</a> under their own commander, served Granada for most of the fourteenth century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ii-of-granada">Muhammad II</a> and his successors played Castile, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> and the Marinids against one another, at times losing or recovering ports such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarifa">Tarifa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeciras">Algeciras</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gibraltar">Gibraltar</a>. The joint defeat of the Marinids and Yusuf I at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-rio-salado">Battle of Río Salado</a> in 1340 ended large North African interventions, but Granada exploited Castile's civil wars: Muhammad V took and razed Algeciras in 1369. In the fifteenth century the balance shifted. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antequera">Antequera</a> fell in 1410, Juan II's army won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-la-higueruela">Battle of La Higueruela</a> in 1431, and Gibraltar was lost in 1462. In the final war of 1482–1492, Castilian artillery reduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a>, Málaga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baza">Baza</a> one by one.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib.jpg" alt="Lisan al-Din ibn al-Khatib" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib">Lisan al-Din ibn al-Khatib</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alhambra_104.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Schluchtenbummler, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid</a> ruler, who styled himself sultan and used the title <em>amir al-muslimin</em>, governed as an absolute monarch drawn from the descendants of Muhammad I, but there was no fixed rule of succession, and depositions, murders and rival claims were frequent. Several rulers held power more than once, among them Muhammad V, who was overthrown in 1359 and restored in 1362 with Castilian help. The chief minister, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, sometimes held the double title <em>dhu al-wizaratayn</em>, combining civil and military functions, and powerful viziers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib">Lisan al-Din ibn al-Khatib</a> under Muhammad V shaped policy. A chancery of skilled secretaries produced diplomatic letters in elaborate Arabic prose, and the chief judge of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, the <em>qadi al-jama'a</em>, headed a judiciary applying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki</a> law. Provinces centred on Málaga, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadix">Guadix</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a> were governed by appointed officials, often relatives of the ruler. Relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> were managed through truces, tribute paid in gold and the regular exchange of envoys, and at times the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">emirs</a> were formally <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassals</a> of the Castilian king. Great families such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abencerrajes">Abencerrajes</a> (Banu Sarraj) gained strong influence at court in the fifteenth century, and their rivalries with other clans and with the ruling house fuelled the civil wars that weakened the state.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alhambra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alhambra.jpg" alt="Alhambra" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dawn_Charles_V_Palace_Alhambra_Granada_Andalusia_Spain.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Jebulon, CC0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>'s most visible legacy is the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a>, the palace-city whose finest halls were built by Yusuf I and Muhammad V in the fourteenth century: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/comares-palace">Comares Palace</a> with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-of-the-ambassadors-alhambra">Hall of the Ambassadors</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/court-of-the-myrtles">Court of the Myrtles</a>, and the Court of the Lions, decorated with carved stucco, tilework and wooden ceilings. Poems by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-zamrak">Ibn Zamrak</a> and other court poets are inscribed on its walls, making the building a book of Arabic verse; together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/generalife">Generalife</a> gardens and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albaicin">Albaicín</a> quarter it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-al-khatib">Ibn al-Khatib</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a>, historian and poet, wrote a celebrated history of the city, the <em>Ihata</em>, and the historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a> served Muhammad V for a time, travelling on an embassy to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-castile">Peter I of Castile</a> in 1364. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a>, ceramics and metalwork influenced Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and craftsmen trained in Andalusi traditions spread its decorative forms in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjar</a> buildings such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter I</a>'s palace in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alcazar-of-seville">Alcázar of Seville</a>. The fall of Granada in 1492 closed nearly eight centuries of Muslim political rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a>, an event that resonated across the Islamic world and in European literature, from the frontier ballads of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> to Washington Irving's <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tales-of-the-alhambra">Tales of the Alhambra</a></em> (1832), and it became a founding moment in the national memory of Spain.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lucena"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-lucena.jpg" alt="Battle of Lucena" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lucena">Battle of Lucena</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Batalla_de_Lucena.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">JamesNarmer, CC BY-SA 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s fifteenth-century decline came from both inside and outside. Repeated civil wars between rival <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nasrid-dynasty">Nasrid</a> claimants, often backed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> or by noble factions such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abencerrajes">Abencerrajes</a>, weakened the state just as Castile grew stronger, while the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid</a> sultanate could no longer send help. In 1481 the emir Abu al-Hasan Ali, known to Christians as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-al-hasan-ali-of-granada">Muley Hacén</a>, seized the Castilian town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zahara-de-la-sierra">Zahara</a>, and the Castilians replied by taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhama-de-granada">Alhama</a> in 1482, opening the final <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada-war">Granada War</a>. Isabella I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-ii-of-aragon">Ferdinand II</a> exploited a split between Abu al-Hasan, his brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-xiii-of-granada">Muhammad al-Zaghal</a> and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-xii-of-granada">Muhammad XII</a>, known as Boabdil, who rebelled against his father in 1482. Captured at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-lucena">Lucena</a> in 1483, Boabdil was released as a Castilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>, and Castilian armies took the western half of the emirate, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ronda">Ronda</a> in 1485 and Málaga in 1487, whose inhabitants were enslaved after a long siege. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baza">Baza</a> fell in 1489, and al-Zaghal surrendered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almeria">Almería</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guadix">Guadix</a>. Besieged from the camp-city of Santa Fe, Boabdil agreed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-granada-1491">Treaty of Granada</a> in November 1491 and handed over the city on 2 January 1492. The treaty's promises of religious freedom were broken: forced conversions from 1499 provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rebellion-of-the-alpujarras-1499-1501">Revolt of the Alpujarras</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moriscos">Moriscos</a> were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1609–1614.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">Emirate</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, the last Muslim state of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, survived for more than two and a half centuries by balancing tribute and diplomacy with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> against help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, and left in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> the most celebrated monument of Andalusi civilisation before its conquest in 1492.</em></p>

